WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.935
-My first guest is
an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:04.370
and Emmy-winning director

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.106
for his fantastic work
on the critically acclaim

00:00:07.106 --> 00:00:09.242
television series "Ozark."

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:11.844
This weekend he is hosting
"Saturday Night Live"

00:00:11.844 --> 00:00:14.013
with musical guest
Morgan Wallen.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.248
I'm thrilled that he can
actually be here

00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:17.483
in the studio tonight.

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.853
Please welcome, Jason Bateman!

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:21.755
-♪ This is Jason Bateman ♪

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:24.023
♪ This is Jason Bateman ♪

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.158
♪ This is Jason
"Silver Spoons" ♪

00:00:26.158 --> 00:00:30.063
"It's Your Move,"
"Arrested Development" Bateman ♪

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.298
♪ This is Jason Bateman ♪

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.234
♪ This is
Jason Bateman ♪

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:40.406
♪ This is Jason "Silver Spoons,"
♪ It's Your Move" ♪

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:43.510
-Look at that, yeah.
This is it. This is --

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.479
-Pretty pathetic.
-This is -- [ Laughs ]

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:48.180
This is the best we can --

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.683
Jason, this is the best
we can do. We just --

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.419
-You goose all the applause
later, right, in post?

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:55.121
-No, this is it.
-This is it?

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.057
-That is totally it, yeah.
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:58.057 --> 00:00:59.325
You look fantastic.

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:02.595
-I wore my baby cord burgundy
for you today

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:03.963
because we're in studio.
-Thank you.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.264
It is a gorgeous suit.
-If it was Zoom,

00:01:05.264 --> 00:01:07.267
it would be my PJs.
-Yeah, that's right.

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.835
Have you been this dressed up
in a while?

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:12.572
-No, and this has got to be
back in about a half hour, so --

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.407
[ Laughter ]
-I understand.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.310
Jason, you're hosting
"Saturday Night Live" again.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.780
You hosted -- you hosted --
-15 years ago.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.950
-Is that right?
-Yeah, it feels like longer

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.786
because I loved it so much

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:30.290
and wanted to do it
every day afterwards.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.725
As you know, it's such
an amazing --

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.161
cords do not move on
this couch.

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.230
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.731
-There's no slide.
There's no slide --

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:41.067
-My entire underwear
is inside me right now.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.569
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:46.005
I'll work five-person audience.

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.606
[ Laughter and applause ]
I'll get it.

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:49.408
-This is better --
[ Cheering ]

00:01:49.408 --> 00:01:50.743
This is better than
my monologue.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.845
This is unbelievable.
-It's so casual.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:54.681
I saw you on the stool.
I'm like,

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:56.882
I said to the guy
in the dressing room,

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:59.052
"When do you guys start?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.954
He said,
"No, we're doing it now."

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.189
I go, "He's sitting on a stool."

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.357
-Yeah, it's a cool medium now.
-It's so casual.

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:07.059
-It's just casual. You're like,
"Yeah, we're just hanging out

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:08.627
like buddies, talking.
-Jimmy's just --

00:02:08.627 --> 00:02:11.164
he's just like a nice
old pair of shoes, isn't he?

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:13.499
-[ Laughs ] I'll take that.
I love that, man.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.267
I have to talk "Ozark"

00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:17.970
because I haven't talk to you
since you started "Ozark,"

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.873
which is bizarre.
-Do you have notes?

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.675
We're locked, you know.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.577
The stuff you've seen
we can't change.

00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:28.047
-Okay, yeah, no,
my only note is that --

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.748
it's a thank-you note
to say thank you for --

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:31.517
That's one of the best things
I've ever seen.

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:32.952
-Oh, man, thank you.
You are phenomenal

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.454
in this show, dude.
[ Applause ]

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:36.622
Unbelievable.
-You're goosing that.

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.090
-No, we don't.
We're not goosing.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.059
-You'll goose that.
You will goose that.

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:42.462
-Not gonna goose that.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:44.564
-There it is.
-That's the one you wanted.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:46.098
♪♪
And now you've got a band thing.

00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:48.534
Oh, my God.
Hey, all right.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.102
Questlove has seen everything.

00:02:50.102 --> 00:02:51.704
You've seen all the episodes
and everything.

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:52.805
-Absolutely.
-Really?

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:54.340
-Yeah.
-I'm on season --

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.810
-Thank you.
-...season two, episode three.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.312
But let me -- no, no, Jason,
let me tell you why.

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:01.947
Let me tell you why.
I don't --

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.616
-You're busy.
-No.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.084
I don't want it to end.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.352
I don't want to keep -- because
I know it's going to end.

00:03:07.352 --> 00:03:08.454
-I'll buy that.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:09.822
-It's like when you read
a good book.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.991
When I was reading
Steve Martin's book,

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:13.792
"Born Standing Up,"
I was so mad that it was

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:15.461
going to come to an end,
I'm like, ah!

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.996
I just want to keep reading
this.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.831
It is so good.
You are phenomenal.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.599
Everybody --
-Really, really good,

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:21.901
good group of people.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:23.802
And the writers, I don't
know how they keep

00:03:23.802 --> 00:03:27.272
escalating the danger
without killing us

00:03:27.272 --> 00:03:28.608
or putting us in jail.

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:31.010
I mean, we make as many mistakes

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.180
as you possibly can without
getting killed or incarcerated.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.682
-Yeah.
-And we've got more in store

00:03:36.682 --> 00:03:38.584
for this --
-Also you're directing, too,

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:40.519
and just --
you're crushing --

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:42.188
Dude, you're --
do you enjoy that part?

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:44.123
-I love it, yeah.
-...another job for you?

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.091
I mean, no. I mean, I guess
there's a part of me

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:48.060
that's sort of masochistic,
you know.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.629
I like the acting.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:53.632
But it's so comfortable for me
that I'm able to observe --

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:55.601
like, I'm keenly aware that
like,

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:58.103
if that camera had to move
right, now I'd probably wait

00:03:58.103 --> 00:04:00.773
to finish my answer
until it's settled.

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.008
I'm always looking
to, like, help the crew.

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:04.777
Because as a little kid,

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.079
the crew members would
always be like,

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:07.513
"Buddy, you got help me out.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:08.881
I got to get the boom
over there.

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:11.250
Can you just hold your line?"
I'm like, "Sure, sir."

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.253
So I'm a real people pleaser
when it comes to the work

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:18.123
on the set, so I like to,
you know, facilitate shots

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:19.525
and stuff that work.

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.694
So I like the acting,
but I like to pile on

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:24.597
with the other stuff and make
the crew's day short

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:27.366
or the actor's day short.
-Yeah.

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:30.102
Just stays fun
and stays exciting, electric.

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.571
-Yeah, I love it a bunch.

00:04:32.571 --> 00:04:37.509
But this next year --
we're two weeks into shooting.

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:39.512
It's going to be the last year,

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:43.583
but we're going to do 14
instead of 10 episodes,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:45.051
and we're going
to split them up in two,

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:46.452
so it will be seven and seven.

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:50.289
So it will really be like season
four and season five,

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:51.591
but they're shorter.
I don't --

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:53.392
-Then it's all over.
-We do land the plane

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:55.861
in a very satisfactory way,
but...

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:57.664
-You're happy with it?
-Yeah.

00:04:57.664 --> 00:04:59.698
-What do you think it is about
-- because I have a theory,

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:01.133
why people like Marty.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.202
Why they like your character.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.538
-He has got incredible hair.

00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:06.739
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:08.174
-He really is
a good-looking dude, yeah.

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:09.375
-I say that to you because
look at you.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.677
-Look at you. Thank you.
-Right?

00:05:11.677 --> 00:05:12.979
-I got this from Amazon
last week.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.781
-If we just agitate it
a little bit up top.

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.615
-A little messed -- but still
under control a little bit.

00:05:16.615 --> 00:05:19.985
-I can see your lace, though,
is coming up just a little bit.

00:05:19.985 --> 00:05:23.889
No, he's us.
I mean, hopefully, you know?

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:27.226
He's, you know,
a middle-aged dude

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:30.062
trying to get it --
get on with it

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:31.997
with his kids and his wife

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:33.966
and keep them alive
and be a provider.

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:37.836
And then he -- you know,
and he's probably a little bit

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:41.173
dumber than he thinks he is,
so he backs into

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:43.910
some bad decisions, but he
thinks they're good decisions.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.013
And then he's got to kind of pay
the price for cutting corners

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:48.948
and stuff like that.
-I think it's also --

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.816
it's because I think
he's honest.

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:53.218
He's honest, even with the --
saying the awful truth.

00:05:53.218 --> 00:05:55.120
I mean, like, "All right,
you really want to know

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.590
what happens?
How the sausage is made?

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.126
This goes that, that and that
and this and that, that's it."

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:02.428
And you go, I can't believe
you just said that.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:04.497
-The money laundering stuff?
-Yeah, all that stuff.

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.465
-Yeah, we try not to get
the audience into the weeds

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:08.935
too much with that,
because it's hard to --

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.936
I think -- the only thing
I've been able to absorb

00:06:10.936 --> 00:06:12.505
about money laundering
in three years is that

00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:13.939
you need fake receipts.

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.441
If you can get fake receipts --

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:17.242
Guys, we can all just --

00:06:17.242 --> 00:06:19.111
we can all retire tomorrow.
-Just, tips.

00:06:19.111 --> 00:06:20.746
There you go. Fake receipts.
-Fake receipts. That's it.

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:23.449
-Just some tips from
Jason Bateman. That's it.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:26.118
Earlier this week we had
Nick Kroll on the show

00:06:26.118 --> 00:06:27.987
and we played a game called
"Guess the Impression."

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:30.055
-That is a funny man.
-He's a funny dude.

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:31.691
So we just had names come up.

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:33.626
We did not practice
the impression.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:36.661
And then I got --
your name came up,

00:06:36.661 --> 00:06:38.130
and so I had to think of,

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:39.465
how do I do an impression
of you?

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:42.134
-Oh, no. This is hurtful.
This is going to be hurtful.

00:06:42.134 --> 00:06:44.303
-No, it's fun. It's all --

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:46.139
-Impressions are not
complimentary, Jimmy.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.205
-Yes.
-No, they're not.

00:06:47.205 --> 00:06:49.241
-It's an homage,
not an impression.

00:06:49.241 --> 00:06:50.876
-An homage.
It's a beautiful pronunciation.

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:52.211
-Thank you.
-Damn it.

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:54.146
Where are you from?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.146 --> 00:06:57.450
Are you from Montreal?
-I'm from Montreal, yeah.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.452
I'm not even from France,
it's from Montreal.

00:07:00.452 --> 00:07:02.088
From -- yeah.

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.356
I want to show the impression,
and just --

00:07:04.356 --> 00:07:05.858
I don't even know why I want
to show the impression.

00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:08.460
-You did me?
-I think I did you.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.430
I think I did a decent
impression of you.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:11.997
I don't know.
Quest, did you see it?

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:13.466
-I did not see it.
-You did not see it.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:15.368
Yeah, we'll see it
when we come back.

00:07:15.368 --> 00:07:16.969
More with Jason Bateman
when we come back.

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:19.172
We're gonna see it, but --
it's close, the impression.

